From: price@price.demon.co.uk (Michael Clive Price)
Subject: BOT: How do trees drink?

How do trees transport water from their roots to their leaves? 

According to my encyclopedias and biologist friends water + minerals are
absorbed by osmosis at the roots and transported up the xylem tubes to
the leaves.  The xylem tubes are hollow and inactive, water transport
being a hydraulic process.  As the leaves transpire they exert a suction
or negative pressure.  A column of water can be lifted approx 33 feet
by one atmosphere of pressure.  How can a giant redwood, some of which
top 350 feet, draw up sap?  They need to exert a suction of over 10
atmospheres (sometimes an extra 15 atmospheres or so in dry conditions
to counteract the suction from soil around the roots).

According to my Britannica negative pressures of 25 atmospheres have
been measured by a device called a pressure bomb.  A twig is cut off and
placed in a pressure container, with just the cut stem protruding.  When
the pressure in the bomb equals the negative pressure exerted by the
twig the cut xylem oozes sap.

A vacuum produces a drawing pressure of one atmosphere.  How can a tree
(or anything) draw more powerfully than the vacuum?

