From: carlf@media.mit.edu (Carl Feynman)
Subject: What is a Singularity? (was: Technology/Politics)

Jamie Dinkelacker asks
>What is meant here by the Singularity? Vinge's, when we unbobble and
>everybody else is gone? Drexlerian "full-blown" nanotech?
>Anarchocapitalism?

The rate of change of most things has been increasing for centuries now. 
Not only is the rate of change increasing, so is the rate of change of the
rate of change of the rate of change, etc.  This makes it much harder to
understand the future than it used to be.  The Singularity is when the rate
of change becomes so large that we are unable to understand what happens
next.  Note that this is in some sense a moving target: as it gets closer,
we can see more clearly what happens in its early stages.

In most people's models of the future, the Singularity is created by the
advent of a self-improving intelligence, able to improve itself in
considerably less than a human lifetime.  This could be an AI, a
fast-thinking upload, a genetically modified human, or something we haven't
thought of yet.  This intelligence builds a smarter faster more persuasive
version of itself, and that builds another one still more superior, and
before too long everything we have and are is obsolete.  

"Singularity" is a term of art in mathematical physics, and denotes a place
where a differential-equation-based physical theory predicts infinite rates
of change, and thus breaks down.  For example, the current theory of
gravity predicts infinite accelerations at the center of a black hole. 
This indicates that we need a better theory of gravity.

Other technological or social steps, like nanotechnology, genetic
engineering, or crypto-anarchy, are much more comprehensible in their
effects than superhuman self-improvement, and thus do not produce a
singularity in our models.  

My own guess for when we get the Singularity?  About five years after the
cost of computation drops to 10^26 ops/dollar.  And when does that happen? 
In 2038, give or take 20 years.  Always assuming that civilization doesn't
collapse, of course...


