In Message Wed, 5 Jan 94 0:21:07 EST, cards@top.cis.syr.edu writes:

>Kathy asks --
>
>> Do you believe your reputation is in any sense yours?
>
>I certainly do.  I built it with hard work and ethical dealings.
>It is a valuable asset -- in fact, my most valuable asset other
>than my mind.  It is worth MONEY.  I just got a contract from
>a Fortune 500 firm, to do some engineering work that their own
>internal engineering department couldn't handle.  I got it
>100% on the basis of my reputation.
>

A reputation is an abstract aggregation of the subjective valuations made by
many individuals. Your provision of consulting services has a reputation, as
do, e.g., Dodge minivans. Chrysler Corp owns a stock of unsold minivans and
are affected by the minivan's reputation. But in what sense is it meaningful
to say that they own it? If it falters in the marketplace, does Chrysler
have a grievance? Not unless you believe they have some entitlement to their
customer's positive valuation. If I think their minivans stink and am loud
in my attempt to persuade others of my opinion, I may well be damaging their
reputation. This is nonetheless simply active participation in the market.
It is not an offense akin to damaging their property. I claim that your
reputation is no different in kind. Everyone is free to damage it or enhance
it as they see fit. It does *not* belong to you, at least in the sense that
your property belongs to you. This is more than semantic nit-picking. The
confusion of concepts leads to acceptance of the nonsensical legalisms known
as libel and slander which only serve to enrich the lawyers.
