Subject: trans- and post-

Nick Szabo and Anton Sherwood have said things on trans- vs.
post-human.  Anton pegs it on the Singlarity, while Nick
uses the advent of uploading, considering Singularity to
be fuzzy.

I think even uploads will be mostly human, and that's the
distinguishing thing for me.  If you're still mostly human,
then that's transhuman; otherwise post.  Or you could be
a human or transhuman in a post-human era if it was
in some sense dominated by posthumans.

This is *related* to the idea of the Singularity in the 
sense that posthuman and postsingularity are both defined
in terms of unrecognizeability.  Maybe we should use the
word "Horizon" for that subjective effect.  (We could say 
the Singularity is where Vernor Vinge's Horizon was on the 
day he coined the term Singularity!)

There's still another interesting use for "Singularity," which 
is a speeding up of history in general that goes to infinity 
at a certain instant in the future.  Even if things eventually
don't go vertical, still singularity is a useful concept
if, say, things followed that kind of curve over a few
orders of magnitude of difference.  It would be a 
singularity in the same sense that a population explosion
is an exponential even though it doesn't keep going up
forever like a real exponential does.

Besides, if ftl and unlimited computing efficiency and
density are figured out, it might just keep going.  Then
the Singularity would be a certain time on a certain
day that the clocks would just approach more and more
slowly every subjective day, year, millenium, aeon...

The Big Bang is a kind of singularity.  People look
at the first three minutes of the life of the universe
as a whole interesting era in itself...but the first
second was the really interesting time...so-and-so's
specialty is around one millionth of a second after the
beginning...of course that's unthinkably later than the 
first ten-to-the-minus-43 seconds that people are 
studying now...

