Subject: Harry Browne on Politics and Morality

I read Harry Browne's "How I Found Freedom in an Unfree World" over
christmas break and found it great. Sort of a how-to book for egoists.
Didn't agree with all his conclusions, but I highly recommend this
book from the Officially Sanctioned Reading List.

He argues in his book that since your individual effort will likely
amount to squat in the fight to stamp out tyranny, if you want to
make the best life for yourself, you should blow off political
activism. I'd think he would have little against you doing it for
entertainment value, to meet people, etc., but he points out that you
likely have activities that can better serve your happiness than
political crusades to save the world. 

I've been thinking about this on and off for a while, mainly along the
same lines. Reading the book made me think about the problem for a
concentrated period of time, finally long enough for me to agree and
"swear off" the holy crusades. It disgusts me to think how much time
I've wasted agonizing over political questions. Entertainment value
only, from now on, please, and I should probably keep that to a
minimum to avoid backsliding.

As an aside, his conceptual approach to political activism could be
applied to the great Looter's Debate over slurping at the government
trough. Just as your efforts won't topple the government any time soon,
so too, the hogs at the trough individually add only a smidgen to the
government's power, and only a microscopic fraction of that smidgen is
an added burden on you. Some guy who steps on your foot in the
elevator does you more harm than another guy taking a $100K government
grant. I personally encourage everyone I care for to slurp to their
hearts content. 

